AUDIO: 'So I want you to think about what you shipped 6 months ago and how much of it still runs the way that you originally wrote it.'

[01:49] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Slide asking "How did you architect your agent?"
AUDIO: 'Some parts of your code likely survives. But did they survive by accident? Or did you design it that way? Did they survive by design? How did you actually architect your'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent? Did you architect it at all? It\'s okay. Uh did you use a framework? Did you custom roll something? Was the architecture an intentional design that you really thought through?'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Or just kind of evolved into what you have now? These are all various ways of where people are these days. So, a lot of folks have talked about harness architecture,'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building harnesses. But mostly, I think a lot of people draw diagrams and they talk about specific components. Draw just nice lines. It looks very similar like, you know, kind'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of simple. But I want to talk about the conceptual layers when you\'re building a system like this. This is maybe the mental model. Not specific components. So, in my opinion,'

[02:54] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Slide detailing "THE HARNESS" with three layers: Execution (The Brain), Context (The Knowledge), Compute (The Hands).
AUDIO: 'there are three discrete layers. First, the execution layer. I think of this as the brain. It\'s where flow, state, durability,'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'retries happen. Then there\'s the context layer. This is the knowledge. All right, this is models, prompts, tools, memory. This is the layer that changes the most.'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And then there\'s compute. There are um this is the hands, right? This is sandboxes, runtimes, browsers that you\'re automating.'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I think these layers are important to consider for the following reason. It\'s your half-life. And what is half-life? All right, it\'s a scientific term. And it\'s a scientific term for the time'

[03:42] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Slide illustrating "THE THESIS HALF-LIFE" with a decay graph for Context, Compute, and Execution over time.
AUDIO: 'that it takes for your for something to decay by half. And I think that your architecture has a half-life, also. So, prompts last weeks, if you\'re lucky,'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'maybe maybe a single week. Uh the models that you use, months, again, if you\'re lucky. Uh but I think that execution can last years, if you do it right. So, the problem is that I think that'

[04:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'most teams couple everything together. And what happens then is that one layer\'s half-life kind of leaks and drags the other components down. You\'re building it\'s technical debt by another'

[04:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'name. So, my thesis is think in layers. Decouple them. So, let\'s talk about what I mean.'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Teams run into a lot of issues with the approaches that they choose. You might choose a framework, it might feel super magical to get going. You might grab a pre-built harness from one of the frontier labs or wherever.'

[04:47] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Slide on "DESIGN FOR EVOLUTION" discussing issues with magical frameworks, pre-built harnesses, and custom-rolled systems.
AUDIO: 'And or you might custom roll the entire system yourself, and it might take months or weeks or whatever. But I think in a lot of these situations, the abstractions either are not there at all or they\'re too high-level or the'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'layers merge. You know, like orchestration is buried deep inside the chain or inside the framework, and you don\'t know what the heck\'s happening. Uh state might be kept in a sandbox. Uh'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'retries get tangled with prompts, the prompt logic. And what\'s hard is you can\'t re- you can\'t like swap any of these things out without rewriting almost everything.'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I think you need to embrace the change. Know that things are going to change. Know that things are moving fast. But I think also with that is I want to focus on a layer that I think is the stable layer here. Where you can'

[05:38] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Diagram showing "THE BRAIN The harness in motion" with Execution connecting to swappable Context (Claude, GPT, RAG) and Compute (E2E, Modal, Docker).
AUDIO: 'invest and think about and get your abstractions right, so you don\'t need to rewrite a large component every 6 months. So, I\'m talking about the execution layer. I don\'t think enough people talk about'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this. And I define it as the execution layer as being the system responsible for running your code reliably, managing how, when, or whether each'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'piece of work completes. And that\'s independent of the infrastructure that it\'s on. So, what does this look like in an agent architecture? So,'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the execution layer manages the full life cycle. You know, plan, call a model, run code, invoke a sub-agent, loop, retry, coordinate.'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can swap the model, swap the context, swap the sandbox, the execution layer should be able to remain the same. It\'s just the the fundamentals. And that\'s how you, I think, decouple and you build the system that it can evolve'

[06:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Code snippet for `step.run` and `llm.chat` with `model: claude-opus-4`, illustrating orchestration and resumability.
AUDIO: 'for change. So, let\'s talk about what the execution layer has to do. First, resumability. Your execution layer must enable your system to pick up after failures without'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'restarting from the beginning. You know, agents are handling longer-running tasks than ever before. So, they must be able to resume when an LLM call fails, a tool call fails,'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything flakes out. We all know. So, if you have a failed reflect step 38, you should be able to retry, wait, continue onwards, instead of having to'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go from the beginning, and you\'re going to lose tokens, costs, time, work that your agent might have completed. So, for this this to work, a 3-hour run cannot hold state in memory or in disk.'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The state must live outside of the work. So, this means that the state must be durable and external. So, without it, you might cobble together maybe some manual'

[07:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'checkpointing. You might come up with a system that has like a log-based approach where you\'re going to be like hydrating the state back if you have to pick up where you left off. But, I think a lot of those abstractions'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'start leaking into the other layers of your harness, right? It gets harder to change. So, next, a key aspect of the execution layer is that it must enable you to combine a lot of invocation patterns.'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'re going to need crons. You\'re going to need to trigger things with events. You\'re going to need APIs, human in the loop. Subagents or dynamic workflows also must be possible.'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'re going to need to be able to invoke these things synchronously, asynchronously, delaying invocation. And I think the key here is that flexible execution and orchestration primitives'

[08:37] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Gantt chart-like visualization of "agent.run - 2h 14m" showing model calls, sandbox exec, retries, and browser actions.
AUDIO: 'will enable you to build the pattern or the system the architecture that you actually need. So, if not, I think again, your harness logic starts absorbing other concepts like queues, workers, polling, backoff,'

[08:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scheduling. And now you end up kind of a mess bad abstractions. So, lastly,'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'execution needs to provide observability across your entire session. Not just the LLM calls, the tool calls, but database errors, permissions issues, um triggers, performance.'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, the full session trace across your entire run is essential. So, if you can\'t see the entirety of a trace from the trigger through the whole stack, it\'s really hard to debug it, let alone'

[09:30] [scene:concept]
VISUAL: Slide comparing "Sandbox" (stateless, ephemeral) with "Execution" and "Compute" layers.
AUDIO: 'improve your agent and keep evolving it. So, what about sandboxes? Sandboxes are so hot. So, agents need sandboxes.'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We kind of like settled upon that at this point in time. They need to execute code. They need to browse. They need to manipulate files. They might work on disk.'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, a sandbox is ephemeral and stateless by design. So, using it for durability, snapshots, or something in state, I think is an anti-pattern. I think it\'s a difficult thing where the state gets lost and'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re kind of trying to peel pull the pieces back together. So, I think when you have the execution layer separate, the execution layer is what gives the sandbox its context, its sequence, its durability.'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I think of the sandbox as the hands and execution as the brain. So, So, what\'s next? All right, what are the next six months'

